Question 54:
As the user sam, using the "registry.redhat.io" registry, create a rootless Apache HTTP web server container in detached mode with tag "httpd-24" and name "httpd".
- Register credentials: username "admin", and the password "administrator".
- Create "~/www-data/" directory and mount it as persistent storage to the container "/var/www/" directory. Create an "index.html" file containing "Hello World!" in the "~/www-data/" directory.
- Map the port 8080 on the host to port 8080 on the container. Declare the environment variables, HTTPD_USER and HTTPD_PASSWORD, and use "test" as their values.
- Use systemd to take control of the httpd container as a service and make sure it persists through reboots.

Answer
1- Verifying that the container-tools module was installed.
# yum module install container-tools -y
2- If you will create the user, you will enter
# useradd sam
# passwd xxxxxx
3- Connect to the user:
$ ssh sam@localhost
4- Login to the image registry:
$ podman login registry.redhat.io
Username: admin
Password: administrator
5- Inspect the httpd image
$ skopeo inspect docker://registry.redhat.io/rhel8/httpd
6- Pull the image on your system.
$ podman pull registry.redhat.io/rhel8/httpd
7- Tag "httpd-24" to the "registry.redhat.io/rhel8/httpd" image
$ podman tag registry.redhat.io/rhel8/httpd httpd-24
8- Create the "www-data" directory
$ mkdir ~/www-data
9- Create index.html file
$ echo "Hello World" > ~/www-data/index.html
10- Run the httpd container:
$ podman run -d --name httpd -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 -e HTTPD_USER=test -e HTTPD_PASSWORD=test -v ~/www-data:/var/www/html/:Z localhost/httpd-24
11- Verify the container has been created:
$ podman ps
12- Verifying that the data can be transferred from the specified storage on the container with the forwarded port.
$ curl http://localhost:8080
13- To define a user service we need to create the systemd user units directory. Then work inside this directory:
$ mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user && cd ~/.config/systemd/user
14- Create the systemd unit files to manage the container
$ podman generate systemd -f -n httpd --new

Note: The previous command generated a systemd unit file named container-httpd.service.

15- Verifying that the "container-httpd.service" systemd unit file is generated:
$ ls
16- The systemd user instance is killed after the last session for the user is closed. The systemd user instance can be kept running even after the user logs out by enabling lingering using
$ loginctl enable-linger sam
17- Verify that linger is enabled for the user max
$ loginctl show-user sam
18- Stop the httpd container
$ podman stop httpd
19- Remove the httpd container
$ podman rm httpd
20- Verifying that the httpd container is removed
$ podman ps -a
21- Reload systemd manager configuration:
$ systemctl --user daemon-reload
22- Enable the service container.service and start it:
$ systemctl --user enable --now container-httpd.service
23- Check the status of the service:
$ systemctl --user status container-httpd.service
24- Verify the container has been created:
$ podman ps
25- Stop the httpd container then reboot the system to verify that the container is persistent
$ systemctl --user stop container-httpd.service
